const paleoCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The archives', note: 'Ice cores (bubbles = ancient atmosphere — 800,000 years of CO₂ and methane), ocean sediments (shells\' isotopes = temperature), tree rings (annual, 12,000+ years), cave formations, corals. Each archive answers different questions at different resolutions.' },
    { math: '2 · The ice ages\' rhythm', note: 'Milankovitch cycles (19–23, 41, 100 kyr — Earth\'s orbit and tilt wobbles) pace the glacial cycles — confirmed by ocean-core records (Hays, Imbrie & Shackleton, 1976). Astronomical metronome, amplified by CO₂ feedback.' },
    { math: '3 · The jumps: Dansgaard–Oeschger events', note: 'Ice cores show abrupt warmings of 5–10 °C within decades — the ice-age climate was twitchy. Mechanism (Atlantic circulation reorganisations) informs today\'s AMOC-watch (the ocean lesson).' },
    { math: '4 · The past as analogue library', note: 'Paleocene-Eocene Thermal Maximum (~56 mya, +5–8 °C in millennia — the closest analogue to our emissions): no ice sheets, sea level high, extinctions in the deep ocean. The past cannot be copy-pasted onto the future, but it constrains every projection (the climate-modelling lesson\'s calibration).' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/advanced/paleoclimate-and-ice-ages">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Paleoclimatology recovers <strong>climate history from physical archives</strong> — ice,
    sediment, rings, rock. It discovered the ice ages\' astronomical rhythm, revealed that
    climate can jump, and provides the out-of-sample tests that make climate projections
    trustworthy.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the archives, the Milankovitch metronome, the jumps, the analogues" widget="equationStep" config={paleoCfg} />

  <h2>Discoveries that frame the modern debate</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The CO₂–temperature lockstep:</strong> ice cores show them rising and falling together across 800,000 years — never above ~300 ppm in that window; today\'s atmosphere exceeds 420 ppm. The past has seen nothing like the present rate (+120 ppm in a century vs natural +80 ppm over millennia).</li>
    <li><strong>"Snowball Earth" episodes:</strong> glaciation reaching the tropics (~700 and ~2,200 mya), ended by volcanic CO₂ accumulating under ice — an extreme test of the greenhouse physics.</li>
    <li><strong>The Holocene\'s remarkable stability:</strong> the last ~11,700 years — flat, mild, the only climate agriculture has ever known. Civilisation is a fair-weather construction (the cradles-of-civilisation lesson\'s invisible precondition).</li>
    <li><strong>Attrition of the ice:</strong> sea level at the last glacial maximum: −120 m (Beringia walkable, Sundaland above water — the anthropology lesson\'s migration map is a paleoclimate map); at PETM-like warmth: +10s of metres. Sea level is a slow integrator of temperature.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hays, Imbrie & Shackleton, Science (1976); EPICA ice cores; Zachos et al. on the PETM (2001); IPCC AR6 paleoclimate chapter.</p>
</TopicPage>
